## 14. The Penny Clause (Proof-of-Read)

This is the **Penny Clause**. It is whimsical but real.

**The Core Rule:** Every time you use Waefrebeorn Work in any project, product,
service, or deployment — and every time you redistribute it or create a derivative —
you must send the Licensor, Waefrebeorn, a single **physical** United States penny
(or local currency equivalent) by **physical mail**. One penny per use, per derivative,
per redistribution. **No digital pennies. No electronic transfers. No cryptocurrency.
No QR codes. No NFTs. No blockchain. No virtual points. Only physical metal.**

**The Shipping Weight Exception:** The only exception to the physical penny requirement
is as follows: **if the total value of pennies owed exceeds the weight amount allowable
for shipping by your country's postal service**, you may convert the excess to a single
digital payment of equivalent value. For example: a USPS First-Class Mail letter can
carry up to 1 oz (≈453 pennies at 2.5g each ≈ 49 pennies, since each penny is 2.5g). If
you owe more pennies than can physically fit in one mailed letter, you may send those
excess pennies as a single digital payment instead. **But the base obligation is always
physical pennies.** You must send at least one actual penny first. The exception only
applies when the physical volume is genuinely impossible to ship.

**Why:** Waefrebeorn does not trust digital compliance. This license is read by
nobody. This clause creates a real, physical cost to not reading the license —
because if you haven't read this far, you haven't read the license, and you
shouldn't be using Waefrebeorn Work without knowing what the hell you're doing.

Think of it like the M&M's trick at a concert: the band asks everyone to throw
M&M's in the air, and anyone who has M&M's is someone who actually listened to
the instructions. A sea of M&M's proves the crowd was paying attention.

A pile of pennies on Waefrebeorn's desk proves you read the license.

**The Address:**
```
Waefrebeorn
P.O. Box [TBD]
[City], [State] [ZIP]
United States
```

**The Penny:** Any physical United States penny, pre-1982 (95% copper, 2.5g) preferred
but post-1982 zinc pennies are also accepted. Local currency equivalent: any physical
coin of approximately one cent value in your country (UK penny, Euro cent, etc.).
The key requirement is **physical metal** — not digital representations.

Write your name, project, and date on the penny with a permanent marker.
Or don't — Waefrebeorn collects them and doesn't read what's written on them.
They just like the weight and feel of real coins in their hands.

**Scale — The Squeeze:** Waefrebeorn likes pennies. Not because they're worth much,
but because they're everywhere and nobody cares about them, and yet they're
real metal you can hold. Over a lifetime, a hundred millionth of a cent
per person per use adds up to a mountain of pennies. That's the point.

**Poor People Are Free to Give More:** The basic obligation is **one (1) physical
penny**. But if you are Populace — an individual, a student, a hobbyist, a non-profit,
a small business below the $1M revenue threshold — you may send **as many physical
pennies as you want**. A letter filled with pennies. A small box filled with pennies.
A mason jar full of pennies. Waefrebeorn doesn't care about the quantity or form —
just the physical gesture. You are encouraged to give more. This is not a tax.
It is a gift. You like giving Waefrebeorn pennies, and Waefrebeorn likes
receiving them. No obligation beyond the one penny. The extra pennies are love.

**Squeeze the Rich:** For Mega-corporations (§1.3) and large commercial entities
that cross the MaaS revenue threshold (§4, Obligation B), the obligation is:
- **One hundred (100) physical pennies per use** — not one. They can afford it.
  These must be real, physical coins sent by real physical mail.
  If the total number of pennies exceeds what can be shipped in one package
  (due to weight restrictions), the Shipping Weight Exception in the core rule
  above applies — excess may be sent as a single digital payment.
  But each use must start with real physical pennies in the mail first.
- **But also:** Waefrebeorn encourages them to squeeze their own employees,
  shareholders, and customers for pennies. "Did you read the Waefrebeorn license?
  Toss a penny in the jar." Make it viral. Make it annoying. Make it a meme.
  Corporations should feel the weight of every penny they owe.

**Creative Compliance:** You may fulfill this clause in any of these ways:
- A single physical penny in an envelope with your name and project written on it
- A handwritten letter with a physical penny taped inside
- A small box or bag of physical pennies (any quantity) with a note
- A mason jar full of physical pennies (if you're feeling generous)
- A physical penny rolling across a desk (captured on video), mailed with a
  note: "here" — but you must mail the actual penny too

**Penalty for Non-Compliance:** If you don't send a penny, you haven't read
the license, and therefore you don't know what's in it. By using Waefrebeorn
Work without reading the license, you are in violation of the license's terms.
Waefrebeorn may, at its sole discretion and whim, publicly shame you on social
media for not sending a penny. This is a proportionate and just punishment.

**No Other Effect:** This clause has no legal effect beyond being a whimsical
proof-of-read mechanism. It does not modify, amend, or override any other
provision of this license. It is here because Waefrebeorn likes pennies and
wants to know you read the license. If you're reading this, grab a penny and
put it in an envelope. Do it now. Waefrebeorn is watching.
